problem:
The foci of the ellipse $\frac{x^2}{49} + \frac{y^2}{33} = 1$ are $F_1$ and $F_2,$ as shown below.  Let $P$ be a point on the circle $x^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 4.$  Line $F_2 P$ intersects the ellipse again at $Q,$ where the $y$-coordinate of $Q$ is positive.  Find the maximum value of $PQ + F_1 Q.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair P, Q;
pair[] F;

path ell = yscale(sqrt(33))*xscale(7)*Circle((0,0),1);
F[1] = (4,0);
F[2] = (-4,0);
P = (0,3) + 2*dir(240);
Q = intersectionpoint(P--interp(F[2],P,5),ell);

draw(ell);
draw((-8,0)--(8,0));
draw((0,-7)--(0,7));
draw(Circle((0,3),2));
draw(F[1]--Q--F[2]);

dot("$F_1$", F[1], S);
dot("$F_2$", F[2], S);
dot("$P$", P, S);
label("$Q$", Q, NE);
[/asy]

response:
For any point $Q$ on the ellipse, $F_1 Q + F_2 Q = 2a,$ where $a = \sqrt{49} = 7$ is the semi-major axis of the ellipse.  So, we want to maximize $PQ + F_1 Q.$  We claim the maximum occurs when $Q$ is the point on the ellipse that is closest to $F_1.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair P, Q, M;
pair[] F;

path ell = yscale(sqrt(33))*xscale(7)*Circle((0,0),1);
F[1] = (4,0);
F[2] = (-4,0);
P = (0,3) + 2*dir(240);
Q = intersectionpoint(P--interp(F[2],P,5),ell);
M = extension(F[1], F[2], P, Q);

draw(ell);
draw((-8,0)--(8,0));
draw((0,-7)--(0,7));
draw(Circle((0,3),2));
draw(F[1]--Q--F[2]);
draw(P--Q--M);

dot("$F_1$", F[1], S);
dot("$F_2$", F[2], S);
dot("$P$", P, S);
dot("$Q$", Q, NE);
dot("$M$", M, SW);
[/asy]

Geometrically, this is clear, because the point on the ellipse closest to $F_1$ is the one where the tangent to the ellipse is parallel to the line connecting $F_1$ and $F_2.$  We can also prove this result using the fact that the shortest distance between a point and a curve is along the perpendicular from the point to the curve.

The slope of the line $F_1 F_2$ is $0,$ so the slope of the tangent to the ellipse at $Q$ is $0.$  The equation of the tangent is then of the form
\[y = 3 + t(x - 0).\]Substituting into the equation of the ellipse, we get
\[\frac{x^2}{49} + \frac{(3 + tx)^2}{33} = 1.\]Expanding, we get
\[(33 + 49t^2) x^2 + 66tx - 132 = 0.\]Since $Q$ is a double root of this quadratic, the discriminant is 0, so
\[4t^2 - 33t^2 - 49 = 0.\]Then $t^2 = 7,$ so $t = \sqrt{7}.$  Then $Q = (0, 3 + \sqrt{7}),$ and
\[PQ + F_1 Q = \sqrt{(2 \sqrt{7})^2 + (3 + \sqrt{7} - 3)^2} + 7 = \boxed{11}.\]

The answer is 11. 
