Let's first find the rate at which each type of worker paints the house.  A good worker can paint the house alone in 12 hours, so the rate at which a good worker paints the house is $\frac{1}{12}$ of the house per hour.  A bad worker can paint the house alone in 36 hours, so the rate at which a bad worker paints the house is $\frac{1}{36}$ of the house per hour.

If we have 3 good workers and $b$ bad workers, then the rate at which they paint the house together is

$$3\cdot\frac{1}{12}+b\cdot\frac{1}{36}=\frac{1}{4}+\frac{b}{36}$$of the house per hour.  We need this rate to be $\frac{1}{3}$ of the house per hour, since we need the house to be painted in 3 hours.  Therefore, we can solve for $b$:

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{4}+\frac{b}{36}&=\frac{1}{3}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad\frac{b}{36}&=\frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4}=\frac{4}{12}-\frac{3}{12}=\frac{1}{12}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad b&=36\cdot\frac{1}{12}=\boxed{3}
\end{align*}
Final Answer: The final answer is 3. I hope it is correct.

