response:
Let's calculate Cal's expected weight loss each week using the given percentage.

At the beginning of the first week, Cal weighs 244 pounds. According to the doctor, he can expect to lose $1\%$ of his weight each week.

\begin{align*}
\text{Week 1 weight loss} &= 1\% \text{ of } 244 \text{ pounds} \\
&= 0.01 \cdot 244 \\
&= 2.44 \text{ pounds}
\end{align*}

So, at the end of the first week, Cal's weight will be:

\begin{align*}
\text{Week 1 final weight} &= \text{Starting weight} - \text{Week 1 weight loss} \\
&= 244 - 2.44 \\
&= 241.56 \text{ pounds}
\end{align*}

Now, we'll calculate the weight loss for the second week:

\begin{align*}
\text{Week 2 weight loss} &= 1\% \text{ of } 241.56 \text{ pounds} \\
&= 0.01 \cdot 241.56 \\
&= 2.4156 \text{ pounds}
\end{align*}

At the end of the second week, Cal's weight will be:

\begin{align*}
\text{Week 2 final weight} &= \text{Week 1 final weight} - \text{Week 2 weight loss} \\
&= 241.56 - 2.4156 \\
&= 239.1444 \text{ pounds}
\end{align*}

We'll continue this process for all ten weeks. However, since we're asked to find the weight after ten weeks and we're dealing with a repeating decimal, we'll need to round to the nearest whole number.

After ten weeks, the weight loss will be approximately:

\begin{align*}
\text{Week 10 final weight} &= 244 - 10 \text{ weeks of } 1\% \text{ weight loss} \\
&= 244 - 10 \text{ weeks} \cdot 0.01 \cdot 244 \\
&= 244 - 2.44 \text{ pounds} \\
&= 241.56 \text{ pounds}
\end{align*}

Since we're asked to express the answer to the nearest whole number, Cal is expected to weigh 242 pounds at the end of the ten weeks.

The probability that at least two dice show the same number is the probability that at least two dice show the same number plus the probability that all five dice show different numbers.  The probability that at least two dice show the same number is the probability that exactly two dice show the same number plus the probability that exactly three dice show the same number plus the probability that exactly four dice show the same number plus the probability that exactly five dice show the same number.  The probability that exactly two dice show the same number is the probability that the first two dice show the same number times the probability that the third, fourth, and fifth dice show different numbers.  The probability that exactly three dice show the same number is the probability that the first three dice show the same number times the probability that the fourth and fifth dice show different numbers.  The probability that exactly four dice show the same number is the probability that the first four dice show the same number times the probability that the fifth die shows a different number.  The probability that exactly five dice show the same number is the probability that all five dice show the same number.  The probability that the first two dice show the same number is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}$.  The probability that the third, fourth, and fifth dice show different numbers is $\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{4}{6}\cdot\frac{3}{6}$.  The probability that the first three dice show the same number is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}$.  The probability that the fourth and fifth dice show different numbers is $\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{4}{6}$.  The probability that the first four dice show the same number is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}$.  The probability that the fifth die shows a different number is $\frac{5}{6}$.  Therefore, the probability that at least two dice show the same number is

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{4}{6}\cdot\frac{3}{6}+\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{4}{6}+\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}+\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}=\boxed{\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{align*}
Final Answer: The final answer is $\frac{1}{2}$. I hope it is correct.

